// ===================== 7.6 =====================
out.push(h2('7.6  The fault and its mechanism'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open, and the finding is in the instruction: **about half the room will discover that the sentence they brought in 7.1 is the symptom sentence their workplace uses.** ⚠ Ask for a show of hands on exactly that and write the figure up. It is the most useful thing the stage produces, because it means the explanation most of them arrived with was not theirs.'));
out.push(A('**b** Symptom: **1**, **3** and **5**. Mechanism: **2** and **4**. ⚠ Note that the two mechanisms are the two with a **structure** in them — a form without a field, a box without a structure — and the three symptoms are all about **people or things**. That is the fastest test there is, and it is worth giving in those words.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — because it is **true**, short, and names the thing everybody has seen; and the symptom sentence had produced **two years of training** on how to speak to customers, which treated the people answering the calls and left the form untouched.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** That the fault is in **what the eleven of them say** on the calls.   **2** That **four of the thirty-four** involve a promise she does not believe was made — about one in nine — so the mechanism predicts that about nine in ten of the escalations would happen even if nobody ever over-promised again.'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The argument of this cycle is not an argument and that is why it works: it is a bill.** A symptom sentence is true, so it cannot be refuted by reasoning; it can only be priced. _Customers say we promised them things_ predicts that the fault is in what is said, which produces training; the training has cost eight afternoons for eleven people over two years, **and the figure it was aimed at is the same figure.** Give the class the sequence in that order — **sentence · what it predicts · what was done · what the figure did** — and insist on the hours. ⚠ Mrs Khoury\'s remark is worth repeating to any class that doubts this: **it is the only argument she has ever seen work on a committee, because it does not require anybody to agree that they were wrong.** It requires them to read a figure and a cost.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(a fault)_ **Symptom:** _that model always fails._ **Bill:** the model withdrawn from one shop, faults continued. **Mechanism:** hand-brushed adhesive, uneven bead, clips carrying the load. **Share:** eleven of fifteen.',
  '**2** _(an empty field)_ **Symptom:** _people do not read the handover sheets._ **Bill:** a notice, one hour, nineteen empty before and nineteen after. **Mechanism:** an unauthored phrase, two shifts deciding separately. **Share:** nineteen of nineteen in one column.',
  '**3** _(a delay)_ **Symptom:** _the drivers are not punctual._ **Bill:** two reprints and a talk. **Mechanism:** one printed time, two readings. **Share:** forty-one of sixty-two.',
  '**4** _(a complaint)_ **Symptom:** _customers say we promised them things._ **Bill:** four hundred hours of training. **Mechanism:** no field, free text, no search. **Share:** about nine in ten.',
  '**5** _(a thing people are trained about)_ **Symptom:** _contributors bury their findings._ **Bill:** a style sheet, three editorial afternoons. **Mechanism:** a contents page written from the front, never disclosed. **Share:** fifty-two of sixty-one.',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark the **bill** and insist on hours or a count. A bill in the form _we have talked about it a lot_ is not a bill, and the figure that goes beside it must be **the same figure before and after** — not a different one. Item **5** is the one to read out, because the mechanism is a practice nobody disclosed rather than a document, and because the bill is only three afternoons: **a small bill with an unmoved figure is as good an argument as a large one.**'));
