out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Two complaints, one instrument, written on the same day by people who have not spoken to each other. Omar said one is about the results and one is about the workload, and they are not the same complaint at all — and they are not the same complaint and they are the same cause, which is the task of the cycle. The record is twelve words: stated range nought point three, reading nought point four, signed by the morning bench. And the missing word is the cause of both complaints, because the figure has nothing beside it: the morning bench reads it as a guide so nought point four is reported, and the afternoon bench reads it as a limit so the same reading is repeated. Which gives the clinicians an inconsistency and the afternoon bench a workload — one cause, two symptoms, and neither party can see the other\'s. Hiba asked why not, and the answer is that each of them can see only their own end: the clinicians see two columns of results that do not match and the afternoon bench sees a repeat log, and neither has any reason to look at the other\'s evidence. Then the three moves, which are not the moves of 6.7: quote both symptoms in their own words; name the one cause and apply the removal test in the sentence; and show what each party is already doing about their own symptom and why both of those will fail. Yara said what her bench is doing — writing "repeated" in the comment column, about four hours a month since September — and what it does to the cause is nothing: it makes their end visible, and the clinicians will still have two columns that do not match. And the clinicians\' own remedy fails in the same way, because the answer to "which shift should we rely on" would be the morning bench, which is the one applying the looser standard. Both remedies treat a symptom, and the better one is the more dangerous.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the second symptom of the student\'s own fault, or what their mechanism says somebody else should be complaining about. ⚠ Collect the ones who could not find a second symptom and do the exercise forwards with them in front of the class: **who else does this mechanism reach?** The answers are almost always a desk nobody in the room has spoken to, and three or four students will have a telephone call to make.'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** Two causes instead of one. → **✓** the removal test: remove it and **both** symptoms must stop.   **✗** Deciding which party is right. → **✓** there is nothing to decide; both are reading one document correctly.   **✗** Treating the louder complaint as the real one. → **✓** both are symptoms, and the quieter party is often the one doing the more expensive work.   **✗** Praising the better local remedy. → **✓** name it as the more dangerous: it buys patience with the cause.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read – exam practice (B2 First Reading and Use of English Part 7)'));
out.push(A('**1** D   **2** B   **3** A   **4** C   **5** A   **6** B'));
out.push(A('Two things to say about this task, because it is the first Part 7 of the course. ⚠ **Part 7 is a matching task and the answer is always a paraphrase, never a repeated phrase.** Question 1 asks for something that explained **when** and not **why**: D says _the complaint explained why we reprinted in September and nothing whatever about the departures_, and the word _trigger_ appears nowhere. Question 4 asks for a **cost** with an unchanged figure: C gives _three editorial afternoons_ and _the same figure_, while D also has an unchanged figure and **no cost**, which is what makes the pair of questions work. ⚠ And the technique to teach is the one candidates resist: **read all four texts once before looking at the questions**, then take the questions in order and scan. Candidates who take question 1 and then read the texts hunting for it answer three questions in the time the part allows for ten.'));
